SEC. 80001. EXEMPTION OF CERTAIN ASSETS.

This section amends the Higher Education Act to exclude certain assets from the calculation of a student's expected family contribution for federal financial aid. Specifically, it adds a family farm on which the family resides, a small business with not more than 100 full-time or full-time equivalent employees that is owned and controlled by the family, and a commercial fishing business (including fishing vessels and permits) to the list of exempt assets. These changes take effect on July 1, 2026, and apply to the 2026-2027 award year and each subsequent award year.

SEC. 81001. ESTABLISHMENT OF LOAN LIMITS FOR GRADUATE AND PROFESSIONAL STUDENTS AND PARENT BORROWERS; TERMINATION OF GRADUATE AND PROFESSIONAL PLUS LOANS.

This section makes significant changes to federal student loan programs. It terminates the authority to make new Federal Direct PLUS Loans to graduate and professional students for periods of instruction beginning on or after July 1, 2026. For those students, it establishes new annual and aggregate loan limits for Federal Direct Unsubsidized Stafford Loans, with graduate students limited to $20,500 annually and professional students to $50,000 annually, and aggregate limits of $100,000 and $200,000 respectively. It also establishes new annual and aggregate limits for Federal Direct PLUS Loans made to parents on behalf of dependent students, capping them at $20,000 per year and $65,000 in total. A lifetime maximum aggregate loan limit of $257,500 is set for all students, and an interim exception is provided for students already enrolled as of June 30, 2026, allowing them to continue under prior loan limits for their expected time to credential.

SEC. 82001. LOAN REPAYMENT.

This section overhauls federal student loan repayment options. It requires borrowers in existing income-contingent repayment plans to select a new plan by July 1, 2028, from options including the new Repayment Assistance Plan, the income-based repayment plan, or a standard plan. For loans made on or after July 1, 2026, it eliminates income-contingent repayment and offers only two repayment plans: a standard plan with fixed payments over 10 to 25 years (based on total debt) or the new income-based Repayment Assistance Plan. The Repayment Assistance Plan calculates monthly payments based on a sliding scale of adjusted gross income (from $120 for income up to $10,000 to 10% for income over $100,000), with a minimum payment of $10, and cancels any remaining balance after 360 qualifying monthly payments. It also terminates the authority for income-contingent repayment plans and makes related changes to other loan programs.

SEC. 82002. DEFERMENT; FORBEARANCE.

This section sunsets the availability of economic hardship and unemployment deferments for loans made on or after July 1, 2027. It also limits forbearance for loans made on or after that date to a maximum of 9 months during any 24-month period, as permitted under the Federal Family Education Loan program.

SEC. 82003. LOAN REHABILITATION.

This section increases the number of times a borrower can rehabilitate a defaulted federal student loan from one to two times, for both FFEL and Direct Loans, and from once to twice for Perkins Loans. It also establishes a minimum monthly payment of $10 for borrowers with Direct Loans made on or after July 1, 2027, who are in loan rehabilitation. These changes take effect on July 1, 2027.

SEC. 82004. PUBLIC SERVICE LOAN FORGIVENESS.

This section amends the Public Service Loan Forgiveness program to include on-time payments made under the new Repayment Assistance Plan as qualifying payments for loan forgiveness. It also clarifies that payments made under the income-contingent repayment plan (as it existed before its repeal) count as qualifying payments.

SEC. 82005. STUDENT LOAN SERVICING.

This section appropriates $1,000,000,000 to the Secretary of Education for administrative costs, including the servicing of the direct student loan programs. These funds are to remain available until expended.

SEC. 83001. ELIGIBILITY.

This section changes Federal Pell Grant eligibility rules. It requires that foreign income of a student's parents (for dependent students) or of the student and spouse (for independent students) be included in the calculation of adjusted gross income for grant determinations for academic years beginning on or after July 1, 2026. It also makes a student ineligible for a Pell Grant for an academic year if their Student Aid Index equals or exceeds twice the amount of the total maximum Pell Grant for that year. These changes take effect on July 1, 2026.

SEC. 83002. WORKFORCE PELL GRANTS.

This section establishes a new Workforce Pell Grant program for award years beginning on or after July 1, 2026. To be eligible, a student must be enrolled in an eligible workforce program, which is defined as a program of at least 150 but less than 600 clock hours (or equivalent credit hours) offered over a minimum of 8 but less than 15 weeks, and must meet several requirements including state governor approval and federal verification of completion and job placement rates of at least 70%. The grants are awarded in the same manner as regular Pell Grants, but students cannot receive both a Workforce Pell Grant and a regular Pell Grant concurrently, and the duration of study under a Workforce Pell Grant counts toward the student's overall Pell Grant duration limit.

SEC. 83003. PELL SHORTFALL.

This section increases the mandatory funding for the Federal Pell Grant program to address a projected shortfall, raising the amount from $2,170,000,000 to $12,670,000,000.

SEC. 83004. FEDERAL PELL GRANT EXCLUSION RELATING TO OTHER GRANT AID.

This section makes a student ineligible for a Federal Pell Grant during any period in which they receive grant aid from non-Federal sources (such as states, institutions, or private sources) that equals or exceeds the student's cost of attendance. This change takes effect on July 1, 2026.

SEC. 84001. INELIGIBILITY BASED ON LOW EARNING OUTCOMES.

This section makes institutions of higher education ineligible to use federal student aid funds for enrollment in educational programs where the median earnings of program completers are less than the median earnings of a comparable working adult (aged 25-34 with only a high school diploma for undergraduate programs, or only a bachelor's degree for graduate programs) for at least 2 of the 3 years preceding the determination. It establishes a process for calculating median earnings, allows for aggregation of small programmatic cohorts, provides an appeals process, and requires institutions to notify students if a program is at risk of losing eligibility. It also establishes a process for programs to regain eligibility after at least 2 years of ineligibility.

SEC. 85001. DELAY OF RULE RELATING TO BORROWER DEFENSE TO REPAYMENT.

This section delays the implementation of the 2022 Department of Education regulations on borrower defense to repayment for loans that first originate before July 1, 2035. For these loans, the regulations that took effect on July 1, 2020, are restored and revived.

SEC. 85002. DELAY OF RULE RELATING TO CLOSED SCHOOL DISCHARGES.

This section delays the implementation of the 2022 Department of Education regulations on closed school discharges for loans that first originate before July 1, 2035. For these loans, the portions of the Code of Federal Regulations amended by the 2022 rule are to be in effect as if those amendments had not been made.

SEC. 86001. GARDEN OF HEROES.

This section appropriates $40,000,000 to the National Endowment for the Humanities for fiscal year 2025, to remain available through fiscal year 2028, for the procurement of statues as described in several Executive Orders related to building and rebuilding monuments to American heroes, including the National Garden of American Heroes.

SEC. 87001. POTENTIAL SPONSOR VETTING FOR UNACCOMPANIED ALIEN CHILDREN APPROPRIATION.

This section appropriates $300,000,000 to the Office of Refugee Resettlement for fiscal year 2025, to remain available until September 30, 2028, for costs associated with vetting potential sponsors of unaccompanied alien children. The funds may be used for background checks (including name, social security number, date of birth, residence validation, interviews, and criminal records checks), home studies, examinations for gang-related markings, data systems improvement, and coordination with state child welfare agencies. [end of text]


